# flow-layout — section + frame layout for consumer flows

Building a flow leaves you with a pile of {{FRAME_W}}×{{FRAME_H}} frames that someone has to hand-place into a
sized, named SECTION at the right gaps — every build. This skill makes that one operation. It reads the
flow **grouping from frame names**, creates or reuses one section per flow, packs the frames inside at the
{{SYSTEM_NAME}} standard spacing, sizes each section to fit, and stacks the sections on the page so they never
overlap. It runs on the **consumer file `{{CONSUMER_FIGMA_KEY}}`** (or whichever consumer file is in
scope) via `use_figma`.

It's the automation of **`skills/screens.md` → "Section organization"** — same math, made
repeatable and idempotent. Load `skills/figma-gotchas.md` first; the steps cite its gotchas.

> Scope: this skill arranges FRAMES and SECTIONS — geometry + grouping only. It does NOT build screen
> content (that's `ui-designer` composing {{SYSTEM_NAME}}) and it does NOT touch {{SYSTEM_NAME}} component internals (DS
> context). It will rename a section to match its flow key, but it never renames your frames — naming
> is the designer's contract (see "Naming convention" below).

---

## The two modes

| Mode | When | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| **Place new** | `ui-designer` just built the N frames of a flow and they're loose on the page | Group by name → create one sized + named section per flow → append frames → set section-relative coords → resize section. |
| **Re-flow existing** | Frames already live in the file, scattered / overlapping / in stale sections | Same, but **reuses** an existing section whose name matches the flow key (moves frames in, re-packs), and re-stacks all touched sections so none overlap. Idempotent. |

Both share one engine — the only difference is "create section" vs "find-or-create section." Default to
**re-flow** when a section already exists for the flow; it's the safe superset.

---

## Naming convention (the grouping contract)

Grouping is **deterministic from frame names** — no inference, no proximity guessing (chosen so it's
safe on a shared file). The rule:

- **Section key = the substring before the first ` / `** in the frame name (the *flow*).
  - `Login / Welcome Back / Default` → flow **`Login`**
  - `Login / Reset Form / Error` → flow **`Login`**
  - `Profile / Default` → flow **`Profile`** (a one-screen "flow" = its own section; states sit side by side)
- **Within-section order = ascending by the remainder** of the name (everything after the first ` / `).
  - Force an explicit order with a zero-padded step prefix in the remainder:
    `Login / 01 Welcome Back / Default`, `Login / 02 Reset Form / Default`, … (string sort handles `01 < 02`).
- **Frames with no ` / `** are **not** grouped — the skill leaves them where they are and reports them.
  Don't guess a flow for an unnamed frame; ask the designer to name it (ties to the global
  `figma-screen-naming` skill).

This is the same `<Screen> / <State>` shape already used in `screens.md` (`"Profile / Default"`),
extended one level to `<Flow> / <Screen> / <State>` for multi-screen flows.

---

## The layout math ({{SYSTEM_NAME}} standard, from screens.md)

Per-frame slot: **{{FRAME_W}}×{{FRAME_H}}**, gap **80**, section padding **80** on all sides. With `maxPerRow`
columns (default **6** — wrap to a grid past that so a long flow doesn't become one endless strip):

```
cols      = min(N, maxPerRow)
rows      = ceil(N / cols)
colPitch  = {{FRAME_W}} + 80
rowPitch  = {{FRAME_H}} + 80
sectionW  = 80 + cols*{{FRAME_W}} + (cols-1)*80 + 80
sectionH  = 80 + rows*{{FRAME_H}} + (rows-1)*80 + 80      // single row → 80 + {{FRAME_H}} + 80
frame i   at section-relative (80 + (i % cols)*colPitch, 80 + floor(i / cols)*rowPitch)
```

Sections are stacked **top-to-bottom** in flow-key order at a fixed page x, with a **160** gap between
sections, so re-running can't overlap them.

---

## Pre-flight

1. **Confirm the grouping.** Pull the candidate frames (the loose ones for "place new", or the whole
   page for "re-flow") and print the derived groups: `flow key → [frame names, in placement order]`,
   plus any **ungrouped** (no ` / `) frames. This is the plan — the designer should recognize their flows.
2. **Decide reuse vs create per flow.** For each flow key, note whether a SECTION of that name already
   exists (re-flow into it) or must be created (place new).
3. **Plan Mode for the write.** Section creation + frame moves mutate the shared consumer file. Plan the
   per-flow list in `yaml` (flow key · create|reuse · frame count · grid cols×rows · section W×H) and
   **approve before mutating**. (Plan Mode before any consumer-file write.)

## The procedure

```
group by name  →  find-or-create section  →  append frames  →  pack + resize  →  re-stack  →  verify
   (contract)       (reuse if name matches)    section-relative   (the math)       (no overlap)  (screenshot)
```

1. **Group** frames by flow key; sort each group by the name remainder.
2. **Find-or-create** the section per flow key:
   `let s = figma.currentPage.findOne(n => n.type === "SECTION" && n.name === key) || figma.createSection();`
   then `s.name = key;`
3. **Append each frame, THEN set section-relative coords.** `SECTION.appendChild` reinterprets the
   child's x/y as **section-relative** (see the section-append gotcha in `figma-gotchas.md`) — so append
   first, *then* assign the computed slot. Setting coords before appending puts the frame in the wrong place.
4. **Pack + resize** with the math above: `section.resizeWithoutConstraints(sectionW, sectionH);`
5. **Re-stack** all touched sections top-to-bottom at the fixed page x with the 160 gap (prevents the
   "sections overlap after re-run" failure).
6. **Render-verify** — `figma_get_component_image` / REST screenshot of the page to confirm sections are
   sized, named, and non-overlapping with frames at correct gaps. Prefer the REST image:
   `figma_capture_screenshot` can hang the Desktop Bridge right after writes.

## Reference implementation (run via `use_figma`)

Self-contained — paste, set the inputs, run. Works for both modes (find-or-create handles reuse).

```javascript
async function layoutFlows({ maxPerRow = 6, pageX = 0, pageY = 0, sectionGap = 160, onlyFrames = null } = {}) {
  const FRAME_W = {{FRAME_W}}, FRAME_H = {{FRAME_H}}, GAP = 80, PAD = 80;
  await figma.loadAllPagesAsync();
  const page = figma.currentPage;

  // 1. collect candidate frames. onlyFrames = explicit node-id list ("place new"); otherwise
  //    sweep the page: loose top-level FRAMEs AND frames already one level inside SECTIONs, so a
  //    RE-FLOW (frames already sectioned) and the idempotent re-run both find their frames.
  //    (One level only — does not recurse nested wrapper sections, e.g. plugin-managed wrappers.)
  let frames;
  if (onlyFrames) {
    frames = onlyFrames.map(id => figma.getNodeById(id)).filter(Boolean);
  } else {
    frames = [];
    for (const child of page.children) {
      if (child.type === "FRAME") frames.push(child);
      else if (child.type === "SECTION") {
        for (const gc of child.children) if (gc.type === "FRAME") frames.push(gc);
      }
    }
  }

  // 2. group by flow key = substring before first " / "
  const groups = new Map(), ungrouped = [];
  for (const f of frames) {
    const i = f.name.indexOf(" / ");
    if (i === -1) { ungrouped.push(f.name); continue; }
    const key = f.name.slice(0, i).trim();
    (groups.get(key) || groups.set(key, []).get(key)).push(f);
  }

  // 3. lay out each flow into its (reused or created) section
  let cursorY = pageY;
  const report = [];
  for (const key of [...groups.keys()].sort()) {
    const list = groups.get(key).sort((a, b) =>
      a.name.slice(a.name.indexOf(" / ")).localeCompare(b.name.slice(b.name.indexOf(" / "))));
    const N = list.length;
    const cols = Math.min(N, maxPerRow), rows = Math.ceil(N / cols);
    const sectionW = PAD + cols * FRAME_W + (cols - 1) * GAP + PAD;
    const sectionH = PAD + rows * FRAME_H + (rows - 1) * GAP + PAD;

    let section = page.findOne(n => n.type === "SECTION" && n.name === key);
    const created = !section;
    if (!section) { section = figma.createSection(); section.name = key; }
    section.x = pageX; section.y = cursorY;

    list.forEach((f, idx) => {
      section.appendChild(f);                       // coords become section-relative AFTER this (append gotcha)
      f.x = PAD + (idx % cols) * (FRAME_W + GAP);
      f.y = PAD + Math.floor(idx / cols) * (FRAME_H + GAP);
    });
    section.resizeWithoutConstraints(sectionW, sectionH);

    cursorY += sectionH + sectionGap;               // re-stack: never overlap
    report.push({ flow: key, action: created ? "created" : "reused", frames: N, grid: `${cols}×${rows}`, size: `${sectionW}×${sectionH}` });
  }
  return { report, ungrouped };
}

// PLACE NEW (only the frames ui-designer just built):
//   await layoutFlows({ onlyFrames: ["123:45", "123:46", "123:47"] });
// RE-FLOW EVERYTHING on the current page:
//   await layoutFlows();
```

## Report (what to return)

A per-flow table + the ungrouped list:

| Flow key | Action | Frames | Grid (cols×rows) | Section W×H | Verified |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Flow A | reused | 11 | 6×2 | (per math) | ✓ |
| Flow B | created | 6 | 6×1 | (per math) | ✓ |

- **Ungrouped frames** (no ` / `) — list them; they were left in place, not moved. Recommend a name.
- **Notes** — any frame that wasn't a standard {{FRAME_W}}×{{FRAME_H}} frame (it's still placed, but flag the
  size), or a section-name collision you reused.

## Common traps (from the gotchas + this math)

- **Setting coords before `appendChild`** → frame lands wrong. Append FIRST, then assign (section-append
  gotcha in `figma-gotchas.md`).
- **Re-running without re-stacking** → sections overlap. Always re-compute `cursorY` from section heights.
- **Renaming frames to force grouping** → out of scope. Grouping is read from names; fix the *names*
  (via `figma-screen-naming`), don't have this skill invent them.
- **Skipping Plan Mode** → it's a consumer-file write. Plan + approve first.
- **`figma_capture_screenshot` right after the writes** can hang the Bridge — verify with the REST image.

## When to use which surface

- **This skill, interactively** — drive one flow / one page at a time and watch each pack + verify.
- **`ui-designer`** can call it as the final step of a build (the "place new" mode) so a freshly-built
  flow lands in its section automatically — no hand-sectioning.
- A file-wide relayout sweep across many pages is a natural `ui-designer` (or `/consumer-audit`) job;
  this skill is the engine it runs.
